1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,134
An engine pulls coaches.

2
00:00:02,134 --> 00:00:04,436
A lift rides with a counterweight.

3
00:00:04,436 --> 00:00:06,808
A flag climbs its pole on a rope.

4
00:00:07,208 --> 00:00:10,568
Each time, bodies are tied
together, and they move as one.

5
00:00:11,018 --> 00:00:15,756
At his study table, Aarav ties a small
bag to a toy trolley, over a pulley

6
00:00:15,756 --> 00:00:16,749
at the edge.

7
00:00:17,099 --> 00:00:19,602
The bag falls, dragging the trolley.

8
00:00:19,602 --> 00:00:21,039
He doubles the bag.

9
00:00:21,039 --> 00:00:23,402
Does the trolley go twice as fast?

10
00:00:23,402 --> 00:00:24,302
No.

11
00:00:23,861 --> 00:00:24,761
Why?

12
00:00:24,870 --> 00:00:27,376
To find out, we solve connected bodies.

13
00:00:27,976 --> 00:00:32,025
Connected bodies are tied by a
light string that does not stretch.

14
00:00:32,375 --> 00:00:37,223
It does not stretch, so both move together,
with the same size of acceleration.

15
00:00:37,623 --> 00:00:40,866
It is light, so the tension
is the same all along it.

16
00:00:40,866 --> 00:00:42,348
We met that in tension.

17
00:00:42,748 --> 00:00:47,405
The method: one free-body diagram per
body, and the second law for each.

18
00:00:48,055 --> 00:00:49,546
To the motion notebook.

19
00:00:49,546 --> 00:00:53,609
An Atwood machine: two blocks on
one string, over a smooth pulley.

20
00:00:53,959 --> 00:00:56,136
Three kilograms on each side.

21
00:00:56,136 --> 00:00:57,036
Let go.

22
00:00:56,920 --> 00:00:58,085
Nothing moves.

23
00:00:58,485 --> 00:01:00,787
Make the left block five kilograms.

24
00:01:00,787 --> 00:01:03,548
Now it falls, and the right block rises.

25
00:01:03,948 --> 00:01:05,144
Slow-mo replay.

26
00:01:05,144 --> 00:01:06,044
Freeze.

27
00:01:05,882 --> 00:01:08,353
Each block gets its own diagram.

28
00:01:08,703 --> 00:01:11,323
Left: forty nine newtons down, T up.

29
00:01:11,323 --> 00:01:14,247
Right: twenty nine point four down, T up.

30
00:01:14,647 --> 00:01:16,234
One string, so one T.

31
00:01:16,234 --> 00:01:19,650
And one a: the left block
down, the right block up.

32
00:01:20,050 --> 00:01:23,551
a is two point four five
metres per second squared.

33
00:01:23,551 --> 00:01:26,413
T is thirty six point seven five newtons.

34
00:01:27,013 --> 00:01:29,243
Now nine kilograms against three.

35
00:01:29,243 --> 00:01:31,229
a jumps to four point nine.

36
00:01:31,229 --> 00:01:32,172
Half of g.

37
00:01:32,572 --> 00:01:35,632
On the graph, a climbs
with the mass ratio.

38
00:01:35,632 --> 00:01:37,343
But it never reaches g.

39
00:01:37,993 --> 00:01:40,121
Now the rule, one step at a time.

40
00:01:40,471 --> 00:01:41,371
Step one.

41
00:01:41,344 --> 00:01:43,633
Heavy block, down is positive.

42
00:01:43,633 --> 00:01:46,125
m one g minus T equals m one a.

43
00:01:46,875 --> 00:01:47,807
Step two.

44
00:01:47,807 --> 00:01:50,138
Light block, up is positive.

45
00:01:50,138 --> 00:01:52,799
T minus m two g equals m two a.

46
00:01:53,549 --> 00:01:54,557
Step three.

47
00:01:54,557 --> 00:01:55,457
Add them.

48
00:01:55,455 --> 00:01:56,693
The T's cancel.

49
00:01:56,693 --> 00:02:01,402
a equals m one minus m two,
times g, over m one plus m two.

50
00:02:02,152 --> 00:02:03,133
Step four.

51
00:02:03,133 --> 00:02:04,473
Put a back in.

52
00:02:04,473 --> 00:02:08,631
T equals two m one m two
g, over m one plus m two.

53
00:02:09,381 --> 00:02:10,356
Step five.

54
00:02:10,356 --> 00:02:14,862
Check: equal masses give a
equals zero, and T equals m g.

55
00:02:15,612 --> 00:02:16,635
Remember this.

56
00:02:16,635 --> 00:02:18,911
One string means one a and one T.

57
00:02:18,911 --> 00:02:21,915
Write the second law
for each body, then add.

58
00:02:22,615 --> 00:02:24,764
Why is T between the two weights?

59
00:02:24,764 --> 00:02:25,764
Link by link.

60
00:02:26,114 --> 00:02:29,837
The heavy block speeds up
downward, so its weight beats T.

61
00:02:29,837 --> 00:02:31,407
T is under forty nine.

62
00:02:31,807 --> 00:02:35,498
The light block speeds up
upward, so T beats its weight.

63
00:02:35,498 --> 00:02:37,746
T is over twenty nine point four.

64
00:02:38,146 --> 00:02:39,954
So T sits between them.

65
00:02:39,954 --> 00:02:42,517
Thirty six point seven five newtons.

66
00:02:43,167 --> 00:02:44,297
Side by side.

67
00:02:44,297 --> 00:02:48,053
Aarav's two kilogram trolley,
with a half kilogram bag.

68
00:02:48,053 --> 00:02:49,947
a is one point nine six.

69
00:02:50,347 --> 00:02:52,384
Double the bag to one kilogram.

70
00:02:52,384 --> 00:02:55,440
The pull doubles, but
the total mass grows too.

71
00:02:55,440 --> 00:02:57,581
a is only three point two seven.

72
00:02:58,181 --> 00:02:59,193
Now you try.

73
00:02:59,193 --> 00:03:02,279
Is the string tension
equal to the bag's weight?

74
00:03:02,279 --> 00:03:03,537
Pause and decide.

75
00:03:06,737 --> 00:03:07,637
Less!

76
00:03:07,298 --> 00:03:11,015
The bag speeds up downward,
so its weight must beat T.

77
00:03:11,015 --> 00:03:14,322
T is three point nine
two, not four point nine.

78
00:03:14,972 --> 00:03:15,956
Another pair.

79
00:03:15,956 --> 00:03:20,185
A four kilogram and a two kilogram
block, tied on a smooth floor.

80
00:03:20,585 --> 00:03:23,639
Pull the four kilogram
block with eighteen newtons.

81
00:03:23,639 --> 00:03:24,551
a is three.

82
00:03:24,551 --> 00:03:27,958
The string drags the two
kilogram block, so T is six.

83
00:03:28,358 --> 00:03:29,274
Your turn.

84
00:03:29,274 --> 00:03:32,728
Same eighteen newtons, on
the two kilogram block.

85
00:03:32,728 --> 00:03:34,033
Is T still six?

86
00:03:34,033 --> 00:03:35,381
Pause and decide.

87
00:03:38,581 --> 00:03:39,481
No!

88
00:03:39,025 --> 00:03:40,107
Twelve newtons.

89
00:03:40,107 --> 00:03:44,684
a is still three, but now the string
must drag the four kilogram block.

90
00:03:45,334 --> 00:03:47,070
Now, one worked example.

91
00:03:47,420 --> 00:03:51,717
An engine pulls three coaches on a
smooth track with thirty newtons.

92
00:03:51,717 --> 00:03:53,832
Five, three and two kilograms.

93
00:03:53,832 --> 00:03:55,189
Find both tensions.

94
00:03:55,689 --> 00:03:57,364
All three share one a.

95
00:03:57,364 --> 00:03:58,612
Thirty over ten.

96
00:03:58,612 --> 00:04:00,794
Three metres per second squared.

97
00:04:01,544 --> 00:04:05,454
The first string drags the last
two coaches, five kilograms.

98
00:04:05,454 --> 00:04:08,404
T one is five times
three, fifteen newtons.

99
00:04:09,154 --> 00:04:12,106
The second string drags
only the last coach.

100
00:04:12,106 --> 00:04:14,857
T two is two times three, six newtons.

101
00:04:15,607 --> 00:04:20,271
Check the front coach: thirty minus
fifteen is fifteen, five times three.

102
00:04:20,271 --> 00:04:21,171
It works.

103
00:04:21,714 --> 00:04:23,177
Now, the exam trap.

104
00:04:23,527 --> 00:04:26,840
Many students say the pulley's
hook holds both weights.

105
00:04:26,840 --> 00:04:28,929
Seventy eight point four newtons.

106
00:04:29,329 --> 00:04:32,469
But the hook holds two T,
the string's two pulls.

107
00:04:32,469 --> 00:04:34,100
Seventy three point five.

108
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:36,265
Less, while the blocks speed up.

109
00:04:36,915 --> 00:04:38,258
Where is this asked?

110
00:04:38,258 --> 00:04:42,529
In connected body numericals: find
a and T, or the force on a pulley.

111
00:04:43,129 --> 00:04:44,378
Exam level now.

112
00:04:44,378 --> 00:04:49,573
A three kilogram block on a rough
table, mu k zero point two, is tied over

113
00:04:49,573 --> 00:04:52,948
a smooth pulley to a
hanging two kilogram block.

114
00:04:52,948 --> 00:04:53,879
g is ten.

115
00:04:53,879 --> 00:04:55,233
Find the tension.

116
00:04:55,233 --> 00:04:56,535
Pause and solve.

117
00:05:01,935 --> 00:05:03,288
The answer is C.

118
00:05:03,288 --> 00:05:05,270
Fourteen point four newtons.

119
00:05:05,770 --> 00:05:10,299
Friction on the table block: zero
point two times thirty, six newtons.

120
00:05:10,799 --> 00:05:15,322
Whole system: twenty minus six is
fourteen newtons, over five kilograms.

121
00:05:15,322 --> 00:05:16,859
a is two point eight.

122
00:05:17,359 --> 00:05:21,451
Hanging block: T equals two
times ten minus two point eight.

123
00:05:21,451 --> 00:05:23,380
Fourteen point four newtons.

124
00:05:23,980 --> 00:05:25,440
Here is your recap.

125
00:05:25,440 --> 00:05:26,940
Screenshot this card.

126
00:05:30,140 --> 00:05:32,071
Can you answer these without help?

127
00:05:32,471 --> 00:05:33,371
One.

128
00:05:32,999 --> 00:05:37,141
Why does doubling the bag not
double the trolley's acceleration?

129
00:05:38,041 --> 00:05:38,941
Two.

130
00:05:38,528 --> 00:05:42,509
Why is the tension in an Atwood
machine between the two weights?

131
00:05:43,409 --> 00:05:44,309
Three.

132
00:05:44,031 --> 00:05:48,172
Why must both blocks on one string
share the same acceleration?

133
00:05:49,272 --> 00:05:53,940
Next time: on a curve, what pulls
Aarav's bicycle round the circle?

134
00:05:53,940 --> 00:05:56,499
Circular motion and centripetal force.
